Apple's rumored smart home hub will have different faces users can choose from, some of which are similar to StandBy screens planned for the <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/iphone-duo/">iPhone Duo</a>. Leaker pdfu <a href="https://x.com/itspdfu/status/2105418868391023008?s=20">shared setup screens</a> of the Photo Face said to be for Apple's upcoming home product.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/09/home-hub-photos-face.jpg" alt="" width="2000" height="1125" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1068012" /><br/>
Based on the images, users can select from different photo collections, memories, or shared albums for the Photo Face, and those photos will be shown on the device's display. <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/photos/">Photos</a> are pulled from <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/icloud/">iCloud</a>, so <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/icloud-photo-library/">iCloud Photos</a> needs to be turned on for it to work. Users will be prompted to set up the Photo Face when activating the device.<br/>
<br/>
Pdfu says the Photo Face for the hub is the same as the Photo Face coming to the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;, and that could explain why Apple hasn't made the &zwnj;iPhone Duo&zwnj;'s StandBy mode available in the Xcode simulator yet.<br/>
<br/>
This is the first visual of a home hub face we've seen. The hub is expected to have a 6-inch screen, and it will look similar to an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/ipad/">iPad</a> except for the square shape and the base. The hub is rumored to have an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/roundup/imac/">iMac</a> G4-like half-dome metal base that connects to the display with an arm.<br/>
<br/>
Prior rumors have said the home hub won't have a full <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/app-store/">App Store</a>, but it will support apps and widgets like Calendar, Weather, Music, Messages, Reminders, and more. The device will be able to tell when someone is approaching it, and can distinguish between people in the home to show different widgets for each user. It makes sense for the hub to display a StandBy-like interface when it is not in active use.<br/>
<br/>
There will be multiple idle display options to choose from, just like there are now when using the iPhone in StandBy mode. <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/09/30/apple-home-hub-imac-g4-style-design/">Earlier today</a>, <em>Bloomberg</em> said the home hub interface is "built heavily" around clock displays and photo slideshows, so the Photo Face will likely be an integral part of the device's software.<br/>
